1. Describe your data2. Create the context3. Show the designer <pre><code>var model = SemanticModel.For(sales, "sales", "Sales")
    .Dimension(s =&gt; s.Date, "Date")
    .Dimension(s =&gt; s.Region, "Region")
    .Measure(s =&gt; s.Amount, "Revenue", format: "#,##0 €");
</code></pre> <pre><code>Context = EasyBIContext.Create()
    .AddModel(model)
    .UseRenderer(new LiveCharts2Renderer());
</code></pre> <pre><code>&lt;bi:ReportDesigner Context="{Binding Context}" Visual="{Binding Visual}" /&gt;
</code></pre>

.NET CLIPackageReference <pre><code>dotnet add package EasyBI.Wpf
dotnet add package EasyBI.Renderers.LiveCharts2
</code></pre> <pre><code>&lt;PackageReference Include="EasyBI.Wpf" Version="*" /&gt;
&lt;PackageReference Include="EasyBI.Renderers.LiveCharts2" Version="*" /&gt;
</code></pre> <p><code>EasyBI.Wpf</code> brings <code>EasyBI.Core</code> and <code>EasyBI.Abstractions</code>. Pick the charting library you prefer: <code>EasyBI.Renderers.OxyPlot</code> draws the same charts with OxyPlot.</p> <p>Your application uses WPF UI or Material Design?</p> <p>Add <code>EasyBI.Themes.WpfUi</code> or <code>EasyBI.Themes.MaterialDesign</code>: EasyBI then follows its colors, light or dark. See Theming.</p>

Behavior What users get <code>AddButtonsBehavior</code> "+" on the right and bottom edges of an item, between lines and between bands <code>FreeSpaceBehavior</code> A placeholder in each free space, to add an item there <code>FieldDropBehavior</code> Visuals made from fields dragged from a <code>FieldList</code> <code>MoveBehavior</code> The grip to drag an item, with live room-making, auto-scroll and Escape to cancel <code>ResizeBehavior</code> The corner handle <code>ItemToolbarBehavior</code> Automatic size, edit, remove; text size, bold, alignment; image fit <code>SelectionBehavior</code> Click, Ctrl+click, selection box, arrows, Escape <code>KeyboardLayoutBehavior</code> Ctrl+arrows to move, Alt+arrows to extend <code>BandMenuBehavior</code> The ⋮ menu of a band (<code>MenuOpening</code> to change it) <code>BandScrollBehavior</code> The scroll bar and Shift+wheel of Scroll bands <code>TextEditBehavior</code> Double-click a text to edit it in place <code>UndoRedoBehavior</code> Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y <p>They are attached by default. Declaring one replaces its default, to configure it:</p> <pre><code>&lt;bi:DashboardView Context="{Binding Context}"&gt;
    &lt;i:Interaction.Behaviors&gt;
        &lt;bi:UndoRedoBehavior MaxHistory="20" /&gt;
        &lt;bi:AddButtonsBehavior CanInsertBands="False" /&gt;
    &lt;/i:Interaction.Behaviors&gt;
&lt;/bi:DashboardView&gt;
</code></pre> <p>To pick them all yourself, set <code>UseDefaultBehaviors="False"</code> and declare the ones you want; <code>DashboardView.CreateDefaultBehaviors()</code> gives the default list.</p> Your own behavior <p>Derive from <code>DashboardBehavior</code>: it gives the view, its panel and the overlay where behaviors draw their controls. <code>DragGesture</code> handles press-drag-release gestures, with auto-scroll (<code>AutoScroll</code>, <code>ScrollsBands</code>).</p>

Event Raised To <code>EditVisualRequested</code> A visual is to be edited (✎, a new visual) Open your own editor: set <code>Handled</code>, then call <code>Complete(visual)</code> when it closes <code>ImageRequested</code> An image is to be picked Replace the file dialog: <code>Handled</code>, then <code>Complete(image)</code> <code>TextEditRequested</code> A text is to be edited Replace the in-place editor <code>ItemRemoving</code> An item is about to be removed Veto it: <code>Cancel = true</code> <code>Changed</code> After every change, with the documents before and after Save, track, sync <code>DocumentLoaded</code> After <code>Load</code> Reset your own state <code>SelectionChanged</code> The selection changed Show the selection's properties <pre><code>dashboard.ItemRemoving += (_, e) =&gt;
    e.Cancel = MessageBox.Show($"Remove {e.Item.Visual?.Title}?", "", MessageBoxButton.YesNo) != MessageBoxResult.Yes;
</code></pre>

Slot Content Data context <code>DashboardSlots.Body</code> The panel, in its scroll viewer The view's <code>DashboardSlots.ItemContent</code> What an item shows The <code>DashboardItem</code> <code>DashboardSlots.EmptyItemChoices</code> The choices of an empty item (visual, text, image) The <code>DashboardItem</code> <code>DashboardSlots.ItemToolbar</code> The toolbar of an item The <code>DashboardItem</code> <p>The item slots are repeated once per item, so they take a template:</p> <pre><code>&lt;bi:Slot Name="{x:Static bi:DashboardSlots.ItemToolbar}"&gt;
    &lt;bi:Slot.ContentTemplate&gt;
        &lt;DataTemplate&gt;
            &lt;Button Content="⤓" ToolTip="Export" Command="{Binding DataContext.ExportCommand, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=Window}}"
                    CommandParameter="{Binding Visual}" /&gt;
        &lt;/DataTemplate&gt;
    &lt;/bi:Slot.ContentTemplate&gt;
&lt;/bi:Slot&gt;
</code></pre> <p>See Slots.</p>

Only a few visual types <pre><code>VisualType[] allowed = [VisualType.Bar, VisualType.Line, VisualType.Pie];
designer.Rules.Add(change =&gt;
    change.Changes(v =&gt; v.Type) &amp;&amp; !allowed.Contains(change.After.Type) ? "Bars, lines and pies only" : null);
</code></pre> <p>The other types stay in the gallery, greyed. To remove them from the gallery of every designer instead, hide them from the context: <code>context.VisualTypes.Hide(VisualType.Waterfall)</code> (see Custom visual types).</p> Some fields only for some users <pre><code>string[] restricted = ["Margin", "MarginRate"];
if (!user.IsInRole("Finance"))
    designer.Rules.Add(change =&gt;
        change.After.Values.Any(v =&gt; restricted.Contains(v.Measure)) ? "Margins are for the finance team" : null);
</code></pre> <p>To hide fields entirely, leave them out of the model given to these users.</p> A title is required <pre><code>designer.Rules.Add(change =&gt; string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(change.After.Title) ? "Give the visual a title" : null);
</code></pre> <p>The title box comes back to the previous title when the end user empties it.</p> Filters fixed by the application <pre><code>designer.Rules.Add(change =&gt; change.Changes(v =&gt; v.Filters) ? "The filters of this visual are fixed" : null);
</code></pre>

Applies to <code>ValueFormat</code> property Fixed style: text color, bold, italic, alignment Tables, matrices, KPIs <code>Color</code>, <code>IsBold</code>, ... Rules: the first matching one gives its color, background and weight Everywhere <code>Rules</code> Color scale: a background from one color to another, between the smallest and the largest value Tables, matrices, charts <code>Scale</code> Data bars: a bar as long as the value compared with the largest Tables, matrices <code>Bar</code> Icons: arrows, triangles or circles for high, middle and low values Tables, matrices, KPIs, gauges <code>Icons</code> <pre><code>new MeasureRef("Amount")
{
    Format = new ValueFormat
    {
        Rules =
        [
            new FormatRule { Operator = FilterOperator.LessThan, Value = 1000, Color = ThemeColors.Error, IsBold = true },
        ],
        Bar = new DataBar(),
        Icons = new IconSet { Kind = IconKind.Triangles }, // (1)!
    },
};
</code></pre> <ol> <li>Without thresholds (<code>Low</code>, <code>High</code>), the values are split in thirds.</li> </ol> <p>A rule's comparison is the one of numeric filters: <code>GreaterThan</code>, <code>LessThan</code>, <code>Range</code>...</p>
